@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_POC.md", "- 2026-11-22 (chat 9): **Benchmarks by memory tier,", "- 2026-11-24 (chat 10): **First benchmarks on a GitHub-hosted runner.** The `Language benchmarks` workflow ran by hand on master `bc6f861` (run #1, both jobs green in 9 min 45 s, `bench` input, best of 10, AMD EPYC 7763 with 4 CPUs, clang 21.1.8, .NET 11, Go 1.26, Zig 0.13, Odin dev-2026-11); the tables and a comparison with the sandbox numbers are in `langbench/LANGBENCH.md` (documentation only, no code changed). The runner is quiet: on compute the median is within 0.6% of the best (C# on fannkuch-redux 1.5%) and the two jobs agree within 0.3%. Compute: Ubel is 1.22x of the fastest as the report computes it (1.21x from the unrounded times), the same as in the sandbox; level with C on spectral-norm, and about 1.23x and 1.25x of C on n-body and fannkuch-redux although both go through the same clang, so the place to look is the IR Ubel emits for those loops (not investigated). GC: Ubel HIGH takes 1.73x of C#'s time with all cores free and 1.32x pinned to one core (1.14x in the sandbox), is faster than Go in both, and peaks at about 27% of C#'s memory. Arena: Ubel MID still cannot run; Zig and Odin took 1.67x and 3.36x of Rust here (1.01x and 1.80x in the sandbox), cause not investigated, and the Rust program uses 8-byte index nodes against 16-byte pointer nodes in Zig and Odin, so that track compares program designs and not only allocators. Manual: C 1.00x, C++ 1.08x, Rust Box 1.22x (Rust was level with C in the sandbox). Not verified: a second run (variance between runner machines), ARM, macOS, Termux, the cause of any gap above.\n- 2026-11-22 (chat 9): **Benchmarks by memory tier,")
)
